 the city of Nazareth, into Judaea, unto the city of David, which is called Bethlehem; (because he was of the house and lineage of David:)\"; Luke 1:27: \"To a virgin espoused to a man whose name was Joseph, of the house of David; and the virgin's name [was] Mary.\"\n[L4] footed on the stating verse and the word and the cross-verses: the angel names Joseph son of David and Mary thy wife in the same row that carries the conception — therefore the row that carries the conception names the father of the house.\n\n**3. The union is stated, and the timing rows sit inside it.**\n\n[L1] Genesis 4:1: \"And Adam knew Eve his wife; and she conceived, and bare Cain, and said, I have gotten a man from the LORD. [Cain: that is, Gotten, or, Acquired]\"\n[L2] No Hebrew word is added here; the English \"knew\" is the canon's stated means.\n[L3] Matthew 1:19: \"Then Joseph her husband, being a just [man], and not willing to make her a publick example, was minded to put her away privily.\"; Matthew 1:20 above, \"Mary thy wife\".\n[L4] footed on the stating verse and the cross-verses: knowing is how a husband and wife conceive, and Joseph and Mary are husband and wife from the espousal — therefore Joseph knew Mary his wife within the espousal, and the child is of his loins.\n\nThe timing rows read inside that chain [L4], footed on the rows below:\n[L1] Matthew 1:18: \"Now the birth of Jesus Christ was on this wise: When as his mother Mary was espoused to Joseph, before they came together, she was found with child of the Holy Ghost.\"\n[L2] synerchomai (G4905) meaning to convene, depart in company with, associate with, or specially cohabit conjugally, standing in \"came together\" in that verse.\n[L3] Matthew 1:24: \"Then Joseph being raised from sleep did as the angel of the Lord had bidden him, and took unto him his wife:\"; Matthew 1:25: \"And knew her not till she had brought forth her firstborn son: and he called his name JESUS.\"\n[L4] footed on those three: the coming together is answered by the taking her under one roof, the union's word is knew, and the not-knowing runs from the taking to the birth — therefore the finding with child is dated before the taking, and the withholding covers the pregnancy after the taking.\n\n[L1] Luke 1:34: \"Then said Mary unto the angel, How shall this be, seeing I know not a man?\"\n[L2] ginosko (G1097) meaning to know, standing in \"I know\" in that verse, present state.\n[L3] Luke 1:32: \"He shall be great, and shall be called the Son of the Highest: and the Lord God shall give unto him the throne of his father David:\"\n[L4] footed on the stating verse and the cross-verse: her words state her present state when spoken, not a vow, and the throne of his father David is already given before she asks — therefore her question does not remove the named father.\n\n**4. \"Of the Holy Ghost\" and \"Son of God\" do not name a begetter of flesh.**\n\n[L1] Luke 1:35: \"And the angel answered and said unto her, The Holy Ghost shall come upon thee, and the power of the Highest shall overshadow thee: therefore also that holy thing which shall be born of thee shall be called the Son of God.\"\n[L2] eperchomai (G1904) meaning to come upon, standing in \"shall come upon thee\" in that verse; episkiazo (G1982) meaning to overshadow, to cast a shade, standing in \"shall overshadow thee\" in that verse.\n[L3] Acts 1:8: \"But ye shall receive power, after that the Holy Ghost is come upon you: and ye shall be witnesses unto me both in Jerusalem, and in all Judaea, and in Samaria, and unto the uttermost part of the earth. [power...: or, the power of the Holy Ghost coming upon you]\"; Matthew 17:5: \"While he yet spake, behold, a bright cloud overshadowed them: and behold a voice out of the cloud, which said, This is my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased; hear ye him.\"\n[L4] footed on the stating verse and the two words and the two cross-verses: come upon is power for witness, overshadow is the presence cloud — therefore neither verb denotes begetting at any row.\n\n[L1] 2 Samuel 7:14: \"I will be his father, and he shall be my son. If he commit iniquity, I will chasten him with the rod of men, and with the stripes of the children of men:\"\n[L2] No added word; the covenant formula is the ground.\n[L3] 1 Chronicles 28:6: \"And he said unto me, Solomon thy son, he shall build my house and my courts: for I have chosen him [to be] my son, and I will be his father.\"; Psalms 2:7: \"I will declare the decree: the LORD hath said unto me, Thou [art] my Son; this day have I begotten thee. [the decree: or, for a decree]\"; Acts 13:33: \"God hath fulfilled the same unto us their children, in that he hath raised up Jesus again; as it is also written in the second psalm, Thou art my Son, this day have I begotten thee.\"; Hebrews 5:5: \"So also Christ glorified not himself to be made an high priest; but he that said unto him, Thou art my Son, to day have I begotten thee.\"; Hebrews 1:5: \"For unto which of the angels said he at any time, Thou art my Son, this day have I begotten thee? And again, I will be to him a Father, and he shall be to me a Son?\"\n[L4] footed on the stating verse and the three occasions of the psalm: Son of God is the covenant title given to Solomon who had a father, and every begetting of the Son by God is laid at the resurrection and the priesthood — therefore the title does not resolve the flesh question.\n\n[L1] John 3:6: \"That which is born of the flesh is flesh; and that which is born of the Spirit is spirit.\"\n[L2] ek (G1537) meaning from, out of, by the means of, denoting origin the point whence action proceeds, standing in \"of\" at Matthew 1:20 and \"of\" here.\n[L3] Leviticus 19:19: \"Ye shall keep my statutes. Thou shalt not let thy cattle gender with a diverse kind: thou shalt not sow thy field with mingled seed: neither shall a garment mingled of linen and woollen come upon thee.\"; Hebrews 2:14: \"Forasmuch then as the children are partakers of flesh and blood, he also himself likewise took part of the same; that through death he might destroy him that had the power of death, that is, the devil;\"; Hebrews 2:16: \"For verily he took not on [him the nature of] angels; but he took on [him] the seed of Abraham. [took not...: Gr. taketh not hold of angels, but of the seed of Abraham he taketh hold]\"; Hebrews 2:17: \"Wherefore in all things it behoved him to be made like unto [his] brethren, that he might be a merciful and faithful high priest in things [pertaining] to God, to make reconciliation for the sins of the people.\"\n[L4] footed on the stating verse and the word and the cross-verses: flesh is born of flesh, mingled kinds are forbidden, and he took part of the same flesh and was made like his brethren in all things — therefore \"of the Holy Ghost\" read as a begetting of flesh joins what the text keeps apart.\n\nJoseph's intent states two coordinate grounds, not a verdict on begetting [L4], footed on the rows: [L1] Matthew 1:19 above; [L3] Mark 7:7: \"Howbeit in vain do they worship me, teaching [for] doctrines the commandments of men.\"; Mark 7:8: \"For laying aside the commandment of God, ye hold the tradition of men\"; Mark 7:9: \"Full well ye reject the commandment of God, that ye may keep your own tradition.\" It fits the link: a wife found with child before the taking is what that tradition would scorn, and his righteousness is not willing that scorn for her.\n\nDECLARED CHAIN\nD1: The promised seed proceeds out of a man's bowels, body, loins, according to the flesh — [L1] 2 Samuel 7:12 \"which shall proceed out of thy bowels\" [L2] zera (H2233) meaning seed, posterity, child [L3] Romans 1:3 \"which was made of the seed of David according to the flesh\" — therefore bodily seed out of a man [L4].\nD2: The father of the child is named Joseph in four registers — [L1] Luke 2:48 \"behold, thy father and I have sought thee sorrowing\" [L2] pater (G3962) meaning father [L3] John 6:42 \"Is not this Jesus, the son of Joseph, whose father and mother we know?\" — therefore the named father is Joseph [L4].\nD3: The reckoning is by the house of the fathers and the union sits within the espousal, with the timing rows inside it — [L1] Numbers 1:2 \"by the house of their fathers, every male by their polls\" [L2] synerchomai (G4905) meaning to convene, associate with, or specially cohabit conjugally, in Matthew 1:18 \"before they came together\" [L3] Matthew 1:24 \"took unto him his wife\" with Matthew 1:25 \"knew her not till she had brought forth her firstborn son\" — therefore Joseph knew Mary his wife within the espousal [L4].\nD4: Yes, Christ had a biological father, Joseph the named Davidic father, the seed honored through his line — [L1] Acts 2:30 \"of the fruit of his loins, according to the flesh\" [L2] sperma (G4690) meaning something sown, seed including the male sperm, offspring, in Romans 1:3 \"seed\" [L3] Matthew 1:20 \"Joseph, thou son of David... Mary thy wife: for that which is conceived in her is of the Holy Ghost. [conceived: Gr. begotten]\" — therefore the child is of his loins [L4].\n\n\n\n[DR1]\n\nCHALLENGER (standard reading) -- ROUND 1, CHALLENGE\n\n# Challenger's answer: the standard reading, that Christ had no biological human father\n\n**Position.** The child was begotten in Mary by the Holy Ghost, took his flesh from a woman, and had Joseph as his legal father by naming and taking, not by seed [L4]. The defender's rows do not reach \"Joseph's loins.\" Several of the defender's own links turn against him.\n\n---\n\n## 1. The defender's own words give the begetting to the Holy Ghost\n\n[L1] Matthew 1:20: \"for that which is conceived in her is of the Holy Ghost. [conceived: Gr. begotten]\"\n[L2] The defender gives gennao (G1080) as \"to procreate properly of the father.\" The defender gives ek (G1537) as \"from, out of... denoting origin the point whence action proceeds.\" Both stand in Matthew 1:20.\n[L3] Matthew 1:18: \"she was found with child of the Holy Ghost.\"\n[L4] The defender's own lexical footing makes the father-word the verb and the origin-word the Holy Ghost. So the origin of what was begotten in her is the Holy Ghost. This is the reason given to Joseph for \"fear not.\" It rests on Matthew 1:20 and Matthew 1:18 together with the defender's own two glosses. The defender reads the row as naming \"the father of the house,\" but the row addresses Joseph and does not give him the verb.\n\n**Press 1 (Matthew 1:20, ek).** The defender asks ek to say \"origin\" in his John 3:6 argument. He must let it say the same in Matthew 1:20. If ek marks origin, Joseph is not the origin.\n\n## 2. The Matthew 1:16 break in the begetting line\n\n[L1] Matthew 1:2: \"Abraham begat Isaac\".\n[L1] Matthew 1:16: \"And Jacob begat Joseph the husband of Mary, of whom was born Jesus, who is called Christ.\"\n[L2] In verse 2 the verb is egennesen, \"begat,\" active, with the father as subject. In verse 16 Joseph is called \"the husband of Mary\" (aner, a man or husband), and the verb changes to the passive egennethe, \"was born.\" \"Of whom\" is hes, a feminine relative pronoun, so it points to Mary and not to Joseph.\n[L3] Matthew 1:20: \"Joseph, thou son of David\".\n[L4] The chain of \"begat\" breaks at Joseph. The relative pronoun is feminine, so the born child is \"of\" Mary, and Joseph is not the begetter. The defender's D4 says \"the child is of his loins,\" but the genealogy that names Joseph stops short of saying that. It rests on Matthew 1:2, Matthew 1:16, and the feminine relative.\n\n## 3. Luke's own narrator qualifies \"son of Joseph\"\n\n[L1] Luke 3:23: \"being (as was supposed) the son of Joseph, which was the son of Heli.\"\n[L2] nomizo (G3543), \"to deem, to suppose, to think,\" stands in \"was supposed.\" It marks the belief of people, not the fact.\n[L3] John 6:41: \"The Jews then murmured at him, because he said, I am the bread which came down from heaven.\" The speakers of John 6:42 (\"Is not this Jesus, the son of Joseph\") are these murmurers. Luke 4:22 is a crowd speaking: \"Is not this Joseph's son?\"\n[L4] The defender's D2 counts \"four mouths.\" Two are a murmuring crowd, and one is a disciple speaking before the matter was understood. The narrator of Luke gives the status of the title in his own voice: \"as was supposed.\" This rests on Luke 3:23, nomizo, and the speakers named in John 6:41.\n\n**Press 2 (Luke 2:48).** Mary says \"thy father.\" The child answers in the next verse.\n[L1] Luke 2:49: \"wist ye not that I must be about my Father's business?\"\n[L2] pater (G3962) stands in both verses. In Luke 2:48 it names the one who has \"sought thee sorrowing.\" In Luke 2:49 it names the one whose business the child is about.\n[L4] Luke 2:48 and Luke 2:49 together place two \"fathers\" side by side, and the child's answer is not to Joseph's house but to \"my Father's business.\" Mary's word is the reputed, household use. The defender's D2 treats her word as settling the flesh, but the answer in verse 49 corrects the referent.\n\n## 4. The finding with child precedes any coming together, and the defender's own union-word is \"knew\"\n\n[L1] Matthew 1:18: \"before they came together, she was found with child of the Holy Ghost.\"\n[L2] The defender gives synerchomai (G4905) as including \"specially cohabit conjugally.\" heurisko (G2147), \"found,\" means to discover.\n[L3] Matthew 1:25: \"And knew her not till she had brought forth her firstborn son.\"\n[L4] The defender's own Genesis 4:1 argument states \"knowing is how a husband and wife conceive.\" Matthew 1:25 denies that knowing \"till she had brought forth.\" So by the defender's own chain, the child was not conceived by Joseph's knowing her. The finding with child came before the coming together (Matthew 1:18), and no knowing came before the birth (Matthew 1:25). The defender's D3 says \"Joseph knew Mary his wife within the espousal.\" But that sentence is the defender's inference. No row he quotes places a knowing before the birth, and Matthew 1:25 places the first knowing after it.\n\nNOT A REQUIREMENT -- rebuts [defender's section 3] \"the withholding covers the pregnancy after the taking.\" I do not ask Matthew 1:25 to say \"not before the taking.\" I point to what it does say: \"knew her not till she had brought forth.\" That includes the whole pregnancy. The defender's scheme needs a knowing before the taking, and the rows he cites give none.\n\n**Press 3 (Matthew 1:19).** The defender's answer was that Joseph's intent states \"two coordinate grounds\" and cites Mark 7:7-9. That answer does not meet the point.\n[L1] Matthew 1:19: \"Then Joseph her husband, being a just [man], and not willing to make her a publick example, was minded to put her away privily.\"\n[L1] Matthew 1:20: \"fear not to take unto thee Mary thy wife: for that which is conceived in her is of the Holy Ghost.\"\n[L2] apoluo (G630), \"to release, to put away,\" stands in \"put away.\" It is Joseph who releases her.\n[L3] Matthew 1:24: \"Then Joseph being raised from sleep did as the angel of the Lord had bidden him, and took unto him his wife.\"\n[L4] MEANS: a husband who knew himself to be the father would have no cause to put her away. The angel's answer to Joseph's purpose is the origin: \"for that which is conceived in her is of the Holy Ghost.\" The \"for\" supplies the reason Joseph may take her. After that answer Joseph takes her (Matthew 1:24). If Joseph were the father, this \"for\" would answer nothing. Matthew 1:19, Matthew 1:20, and Matthew 1:24 carry this.\nThe defender's Mark 7:7-9 is about \"the tradition of the elders\" and \"unwashen hands\" (Mark 7:5). No quoted row connects it to Joseph's intent. The defender's closing L4 (\"a wife found with child before the taking is what that tradition would scorn\") has the verse Mark 7:7-9 and the Matthew 1:19 text, but the link between them is the defender's own addition. Mark 7 states nothing about it, so the inference floats.\n\n## 5. Luke 1:34-35: the question asked, and the answer given\n\n[L1] Luke 1:34: \"How shall this be, seeing I know not a man?\"\n[L1] Luke 1:35: \"The Holy Ghost shall come upon thee, and the power of the Highest shall overshadow thee: therefore also that holy thing which shall be born of thee shall be called the Son of God.\"\n[L2] aner (G435), \"a man, a male, a husband,\" stands in \"a man.\" The defender grants ginosko (G1097) as present state. dio (G1352) kai, \"wherefore also,\" stands in \"therefore also.\"\n[L3] Luke 1:27: \"To a virgin espoused to a man whose name was Joseph.\" She is already espoused, yet asks \"How.\"\n[L4] I grant the present tense. Her state is \"I know not a man,\" and the question is \"how.\" The angel answers the \"how\" with the Holy Ghost's act. It does not answer \"Joseph will know you.\" The defender's point that \"come upon\" is power (Acts 1:8) and \"overshadow\" is presence (Matthew 17:5) does not touch this. I do not claim the verbs mean \"beget.\" I claim that the answer to \"how, seeing I know not a man?\" is supplied by the Holy Ghost and the power of the Highest, with no man named in the answer. That rests on Luke 1:34, Luke 1:35, and the espousal in Luke 1:27.\n\nNOT A REQUIREMENT -- rebuts [defender's section 3] \"her question does not remove the named father.\" I do not demand that Luke 1:35 say \"no father.\" I point to the means the angel states and to the \"therefore.\"\n\n**Press 4 (Luke 1:35, \"therefore\").** The defender says \"Son of God\" is a covenant title like Solomon's.\n[L1] 1 Chronicles 28:6: \"I have chosen him [to be] my son, and I will be his father.\"\n[L2] The \"therefore\" (dio kai, \"for which cause also\") ties the title to the conception: \"that holy thing which shall be born of thee.\"\n[L3] 2 Samuel 7:14: \"I will be his father, and he shall be my son.\"\n[L4] Solomon's sonship rests on a choosing (1 Chronicles 28:6). Luke's sonship rests on \"therefore,\" on the conception by the Holy Ghost. The defender's L3 Psalm 2:7, Acts 13:33, Hebrews 5:5 are about a \"this day\" of resurrection and priesthood, not the conception row. So they do not answer the \"therefore\" of Luke 1:35.\n\n## 6. The seed question: the same word zera stands for the woman\n\n[L1] Genesis 3:15: \"And I will put enmity between thee and the woman, and between thy seed and her seed; it shall bruise thy head, and thou shalt bruise his heel.\"\n[L2] zera (H2233), \"seed,\" the word the defender uses at 2 Samuel 7:12, stands in \"her seed.\" Here it is the woman's seed.\n[L3] Galatians 4:4: \"But when the fulness of the time was come, God sent forth his Son, made of a woman, made under the law.\"\n[L3] Hebrews 10:5: \"a body hast thou prepared me.\"\n[L2] katartizo (G2675), \"to prepare, to fit out,\" stands in \"hast thou prepared.\"\n[L4] The defender's D1 says the seed is bodily substance out of a man. But zera is used of the woman's seed in Genesis 3:15, and Galatians 4:4 says he was \"made of a woman.\" Hebrews 10:5 says a body was prepared. So the Davidic \"seed\" can be through the woman, and the defender's step \"flesh without a man's seed has no row here\" is answered by those rows.\n\n**Press 5 (Hebrews 2:14, 2:16-17).** The defender reads these to require a man's seed.\n[L1] Hebrews 2:14: \"the children are partakers of flesh and blood, he also himself likewise took part of the same.\"\n[L2] The words are \"flesh and blood\" (sarx kai haima), human nature. They are not \"a man's seed.\"\n[L3] Luke 1:35 \"that holy thing which shall be born of thee.\"\n[L4] The flesh and blood he shares come from \"born of thee,\" from Mary. That meets \"partakers of flesh and blood\" and \"made like unto his brethren\" (Hebrews 2:17) with no father's seed named. The defender's John 3:6 (\"That which is born of the flesh is flesh\") concerns the new birth. His Leviticus 19:19 forbids Israel to mingle cattle and seed. It is a statute for Israel's husbandry, not a limit on what God does in Luke 1:35. The L4 \"