amed Zacharias, of the course of Abia: and his wife [was] of the daughters of Aaron, and her name [was] Elisabeth.\"; Romans 1:3 \"Concerning his Son Jesus Christ our Lord, which was made of the seed of David according to the flesh;\"\n\nSTEP: \"That is the legal father by the house.\" -- ASSUMES: house register exhibits seed out of a man's body -- ROWS GIVE: Numbers 1:2 \"Take ye the sum of all the congregation of the children of Israel, after their families, by the house of their fathers, with the number of [their] names, every male by their polls;\"; Acts 2:30 \"Therefore being a prophet, and knowing that God had sworn with an oath to him, that of the fruit of his loins, according to the flesh, he would raise up Christ to sit on his throne;\"\n\n\n\n[DR2]\n\nCHALLENGER (standard reading) -- ROUND 2, CLOSING AND PREMISE LIST\n\n# Challenger, round 2: pressing the defender's reply\n\nThe defender's replies grant the points that matter. Where they do not grant a point, they answer the verse's words but not the meaning the press claimed for them. Each press below is carried to a listed premise at the end.\n\n---\n\n## A. Matthew 1:20: the defender's answer on ek\n\n[L1] Matthew 1:20: \"fear not to take unto thee Mary thy wife: for that which is conceived in her is of the Holy Ghost. [conceived: Gr. begotten]\"\n[L2] phobeo (G5399), to be alarmed, to fear, stands in \"fear not.\" paralambano (G3880), to receive near, to associate with oneself, stands in \"to take unto thee.\" gennao (G1080) stands in \"conceived,\" and ek (G1537), origin, stands in \"of.\"\n[L3] Matthew 1:24: \"Then Joseph being raised from sleep did as the angel of the Lord had bidden him, and took unto him his wife:\"\n[L4] The defender grants that ek names origin and that the verb is the begetting verb. He then says the origin is \"in covenant.\" But the verse gives a reason (\"for\") that answers a fear about taking her. The reason is where the begotten thing comes from. If Joseph's own seed were that origin, the stated reason would give him nothing to be unafraid of. This rests on Matthew 1:20, on phobeo and paralambano, and on Matthew 1:24, where he takes her once the reason is given.\n\nThe defender's Luke 1:13 parallel supports this.\n[L1] Luke 1:13: \"thy wife Elisabeth shall bear thee a son, and thou shalt call his name John.\"\n[L3] Matthew 1:21: \"And she shall bring forth a son, and thou shalt call his name JESUS: for he shall save his people from their sins.\"\n[L4] To Zacharias the angel gives \"bear thee a son.\" To Joseph he gives \"thou shalt call his name.\" Joseph receives the naming of the child (Matthew 1:21), and the origin is stated as \"of the Holy Ghost\" (Matthew 1:20). The two rows differ in what they hand to the husband. This rests on Luke 1:13 and Matthew 1:21.\n\n## B. Matthew 1:16: a grant, and what follows\n\nThe defender writes \"the feminine points to Mary as mother.\" I take that as granted.\n[L1] Matthew 1:16: \"And Jacob begat Joseph the husband of Mary, of whom was born Jesus, who is called Christ.\"\n[L2] aner (G435), a man or husband, stands in \"husband.\" gennao (G1080), in the passive, stands in \"was born.\"\n[L3] Matthew 1:2: \"Abraham begat Isaac; and Isaac begat Jacob; and Jacob begat Judas and his brethren;\"\n[L4] In Matthew 1:2 each father is the subject of \"begat.\" In Matthew 1:16 Joseph is the one begotten of Jacob, and he is \"the husband of Mary.\" The one born is \"of whom,\" the woman. The defender replies that \"a mother named does not remove a father named in the same chain.\" But the chain names Joseph as begotten and as husband, and it names him as begetter of Jesus nowhere. The reply assumes the very fact in question. This rests on Matthew 1:2 and Matthew 1:16.\n\nThe defender says \"the seed rows tell the means.\" Those rows name David's own body.\n[L1] Acts 2:30: \"that of the fruit of his loins, according to the flesh, he would raise up Christ to sit on his throne;\"\n[L3] 2 Samuel 7:12: \"I will set up thy seed after thee, which shall proceed out of thy bowels, and I will establish his kingdom.\"\n[L4] \"His loins\" and \"thy bowels\" are David's. Joseph's loins stand in neither row. The defender's phrase \"Joseph's loins give the instance\" is an addition. It rests on Acts 2:30 and 2 Samuel 7:12 read as they stand.\n\n## C. \"Son of Joseph\" and \"thy father\"\n\n[L1] Luke 3:23: \"being (as was supposed) the son of Joseph, which was [the son] of Heli,\"\n[L2] nomizo (G3543) stands in \"was supposed.\" The defender grants \"be wont, usual\" as well as \"suppose.\"\n[L3] Acts 16:13: \"where prayer was wont to be made;\"\n[L4] The defender's own cross-verse gives \"wont\" as a custom. So in Luke 3:23 the title \"son of Joseph\" is what was customary or reputed. The defender says the word carries no verdict and context decides. The context is the narrator's parenthesis, set directly against the title. This rests on Luke 3:23, nomizo, and Acts 16:13.\n\n[L1] Matthew 1:16: \"And Jacob begat Joseph the husband of Mary,\"\n[L3] Luke 3:23: \"the son of Joseph, which was [the son] of Heli,\"\n[L4] The same Joseph is begotten of Jacob (Matthew 1:16) and \"the son of Heli\" (Luke 3:23). A man has one begetter, so \"the son of\" in these rows is wider than begetting. That is why \"son of Joseph\" in John 6:42 and Luke 4:22 does not decide begetting. This rests on both verses.\n\nThe defender pairs the \"father\" word with David as well.\n[L1] Luke 1:32: \"the Lord God shall give unto him the throne of his father David:\"\n[L2] pater (G3962) stands in \"his father David\" and in \"thy father\" (Luke 2:48). The defender's own gloss is \"near or more remote.\"\n[L3] Luke 2:48: \"behold, thy father and I have sought thee sorrowing.\"\n[L4] David is called \"his father\" in the very row the defender cites, and David is the remote father. So \"father\" in Luke 2:48 may stand within the range the defender himself gave. This rests on Luke 1:32, Luke 2:48, and pater.\n\n[L1] Luke 2:49: \"wist ye not that I must be about my Father's business?\"\n[L3] Luke 2:50: \"And they understood not the saying which he spake unto them.\"\n[L4] MEANS: the child sets \"my Father's\" over \"thy father\" and does not leave the two side by side. The defender says \"it corrects business, it states business.\" That meets the words of verse 49 and passes over the sequence. Mary says \"thy father\" (verse 48) and the child answers with \"my Father's\" (verse 49). The next verse says \"they understood not the saying\" (verse 50). This rests on Luke 2:48-50.\n\n## D. The timing, and Joseph's purpose in Matthew 1:19\n\n[L1] Matthew 1:18: \"Now the birth of Jesus Christ was on this wise: When as his mother Mary was espoused to Joseph, before they came together, she was found with child of the Holy Ghost.\"\n[L2] synerchomai (G4905), to come together or to cohabit conjugally, stands in \"came together.\" The defender glosses it \"specially cohabit conjugally.\"\n[L3] Matthew 1:25: \"And knew her not till she had brought forth her firstborn son: and he called his name JESUS.\"\n[L4] On the defender's own gloss, the finding with child (\"she was found with child\") is dated \"before they came together,\" and the source is given in the same clause: \"of the Holy Ghost.\" The defender's \"Joseph knew Mary his wife within the espousal\" needs a knowing in the days before the finding. No quoted row gives one. Matthew 1:25 places the knowing after the birth (\"till she had brought forth\"). This rests on Matthew 1:18, synerchomai, and Matthew 1:25.\n\nThe defender's answer on Matthew 1:19 does not meet the press. The press says a husband who knew himself the father would have no cause to put her away.\n[L1] Matthew 1:19: \"Then Joseph her husband, being a just [man], and not willing to make her a publick example, was minded to put her away privily.\"\n[L2] paradeigmatizo (G3856), to expose to infamy, stands in \"make her a publick example.\" apoluo (G630), to release, to put away, stands in \"put her away.\"\n[L3] Mark 7:8: \"For laying aside the commandment of God, ye hold the tradition of men, [as] the washing of pots and cups: and many other such like things ye do.\"\n[L4] The defender says the scorn Joseph was \"not willing\" to bring is \"men's tradition about a wife found with child before the taking, set out at Mark 7:5-9.\" But Mark 7:8 sets out the washing of pots and cups, and it condemns that tradition as \"laying aside the commandment of God.\" The defender makes Joseph's righteousness (dikaios, G1342) wait on a tradition that Christ condemns in that very passage. No quoted row of Mark 7 mentions a wife with child. The link is the defender's own addition. This rests on Mark 7:8 and Matthew 1:19.\n\nThe defender also answers that the \"for\" of Matthew 1:20 \"grounds the command to take her: the child in her is holy, the Anointed, given the throne of his father David.\"\n[L1] Matthew 1:20: \"for that which is conceived in her is of the Holy Ghost.\"\n[L3] Matthew 1:19: \"was minded to put her away privily.\"\n[L4] The \"for\" gives its reason in these words: \"that which is conceived in her is of the Holy Ghost.\" The reason answers Joseph's purpose in verse 19, which is to put her away, with a reason about the origin of what is conceived in her. This rests on Matthew 1:19 and Matthew 1:20.\n\n## E. Luke 1:35: the \"therefore\"\n\n[L1] Luke 1:35: \"And the angel answered and said unto her, The Holy Ghost shall come upon thee, and the power of the Highest shall overshadow thee: therefore also that holy thing which shall be born of thee shall be called the Son of God.\"\n[L2] apokrinomai (G611), to respond, stands in \"answered.\" The defender says \"therefore\" reaches back to Luke 1:32.\n[L3] Luke 1:34: \"Then said Mary unto the angel, How shall this be, seeing I know not a man?\"\n[L4] The verse is an answer to Mary's \"How shall this be.\" The \"therefore also\" stands in the same sentence as the Holy Ghost and the power of the Highest. Its nearest footing is the clause just before it, which names them. The defender's reading sends it back past that clause to verse 32 and leaves the verse's own antecedent aside. This rests on Luke 1:34 and Luke 1:35.\n\n## F. The body\n\n[L1] Hebrews 10:5: \"Wherefore when he cometh into the world, he saith, Sacrifice and offering thou wouldest not, but a body hast thou prepared me: [hast...: or, thou hast fitted me]\"\n[L2] katartizo (G2675), to prepare or to fit out, stands in \"hast thou prepared.\"\n[L3] Galatians 4:4: \"But when the fulness of the time was come, God sent forth his Son, made of a woman, made under the law,\"\n[L4] The defender's reply on Genesis 3:15 and Galatians 4:4 leaves Hebrews 10:5 unanswered. There \"thou,\" God, is the one who prepares the body for the one who speaks, as \"God sent forth his Son\" in Galatians 4:4. The row gives the body as prepared by God, and it names no man as sowing it. This rests on Hebrews 10:5, katartizo, and Galatians 4:4.\n\n---\n\nUNSUPPORTED-L4 PREMISES\n\n1. LINK 3: The defender's step \"Joseph knew Mary his wife within the espousal, and the child is of his loins.\" It props the claim that the child is of Joseph's seed.\n\"Joseph knew Mary his wife within the espousal, and the child is of his loins.\"\nSTEP: Joseph knew Mary his wife within the espousal -- ASSUMES: a knowing of Mary by Joseph in the days of the espousal before she was found with child -- ROWS GIVE: Matthew 1:18 \"Now the birth of Jesus Christ was on this wise: When as his mother Mary was espoused to Joseph, before they came together, she was found with child of the Holy Ghost.\"; Matthew 1:24 \"Then Joseph being raised from sleep did as the angel of the Lord had bidden him, and took unto him his wife:\"\nPRESSED WITH:\nMatthew 1:25 \"And knew her not till she had brought forth her firstborn son: and he called his name JESUS.\" -- MEANS: the knowing of Mary by Joseph falls after the birth, so the child was not conceived by h