s 2:30. The \"his\" of that verse is David, who is named in it as the one to whom God \"had sworn with an oath\". I grant the seed is David's.\n[L3] Luke 1:27: \"a man whose name was Joseph, of the house of David\". Luke 2:4: \"he was of the house and lineage of David\". These name Joseph's house and lineage. I grant them. They do not name Joseph's loins as the source of the child.\n[L4] Acts 2:30 and Romans 1:3 name David as the one whose seed and loins are in view. Matthew 1:20 names the Holy Ghost as the source of what is begotten in Mary. Calling Joseph's loins the instance is a further step. It has no verse of its own, and it sits against Matthew 1:20.\n\n**C3: SUPPORTED.** In these rows \"son of\" is wider than begetting.\n[L1] Luke 4:22: \"Is not this Joseph's son?\"\n[L2] huios (G5207), meaning \"a 'son'… used very widely of immediate, remote or figuratively, kinship\", stands in \"Joseph's son\" in Luke 4:22.\n[L3] Matthew 1:16: \"And Jacob begat Joseph the husband of Mary, of whom was born Jesus, who is called Christ.\" In Matthew 1:2 the chain verb is \"begat\" (\"Abraham begat Isaac\"). In Matthew 1:16 the verb of the last link is \"was born\".\n[L4] \"Begat\" runs down the chain to Joseph (Matthew 1:2, Matthew 1:16). The final clause turns to \"of whom was born Jesus\", and Joseph is there called \"the husband of Mary\". A word that stretches over remote and figurative kinship (Luke 4:22) does not by itself fix Joseph as begetter.\n\n**C4: SUPPORTED.** In Luke 3:23 the title \"son of Joseph\" is what was customary or reputed.\n[L1] Luke 3:23: \"being (as was supposed) the son of Joseph\".\n[L2] nomizo (G3543), meaning \"to do by law (usage)… to deem or regard\", stands in \"was supposed\". I grant that the verb alone gives no verdict. The verdict comes from what the same book has already said.\n[L3] Luke 1:35 gives the reason the child is called what he is called: \"therefore also that holy thing which shall be born of thee shall be called the Son of Go